# Catch2 decomposes EVERY assertion so it can print both operands on failure:
# `REQUIRE( a == b )` expands, via INTERNAL_CATCH_TEST, to
# `Catch::Decomposer() <= a == b` (tests/catch/catch.hpp). `Decomposer::operator<=`
# is a low-precedence hook that captures the left operand; the `<=` is a parsing
# trick, not a comparison. But it IS a chained comparison in the grammar, so
# bugprone-chained-comparison fires on every REQUIRE/CHECK containing one.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-24, whole tree under LLVM 21: 8,576 sites, ALL in tests/,
# ZERO in src/ or tools/. That is 70% of every finding in the tree.
#
# Not fixable here. Catch2 already wraps the same construct in
# CATCH_INTERNAL_SUPPRESS_PARENTHESES_WARNINGS for the compiler's own
# -Wparentheses; that pragma does not reach clang-tidy. The only "fix" would be
# parenthesising inside each assertion, which defeats the decomposition and loses
# the operand values from failure output -- the reason Catch2 does this at all.
#
# SCOPED TO tests/ DELIBERATELY, not disabled tree-wide: src/ has no hits today,
# so a genuine `a < b < c` in shipping code must still fail the gate.
#
# InheritParentConfig IS REQUIRED. Without it a child .clang-tidy REPLACES the
# parent's config rather than extending it, so this file would disable every
# check for tests/ while looking like it disabled one. It fails silently -- the
# symptom is clang-tidy printing nothing at all, which reads as "clean".
InheritParentConfig: true
Checks: '-bugprone-chained-comparison'
